@@doc """
Standard `Lens` type declaration.
`Lens<S, A>` is a compiler-managed path capability from source `S` to focus `A`.
Lens values are consumed in the same scope via `Lens::view/set/over/over_result` and are not
transported as runtime values across function boundaries.

Use `Lens` when you want to describe "where" inside a value the compiler should
look or update.
When a struct or record field should participate in Result-style update flows,
prefer `T?` or `Result<T, NoneError>` over `Option<T>`.
That keeps the field aligned with `set`, `over`, `over_result`, `=?`, and
ordinary `Result`-returning helper functions without repeated container
conversion.

- `Type.segment` builds a path itself
- `value.segment` is sugar for applying the corresponding path to `value`

Common path forms:

- struct / record field: `User.name`
- tuple field: `Tuple._0`, `Tuple._1`, ...
- nested path: `User.profile / Profile.name`
- function-form nested path: `Lens::compose(User.profile, Profile.name)`
- enum variant selector: `Expr.Add`
- value sugar: `value.segment`

`Lens` is compile-time only in Stage1.
You may bind it in the same scope:

`lens = User.name`
`tuple_lens = Tuple._1`

but you may not pass it as a function argument, return it, capture it in a
closure, or store it inside runtime containers such as `List`, tuples, `Ok(...)`
or `Err(...)`.

Private field paths follow the same visibility rules as ordinary field access.
Outside the allowed scope, `User.secret`-style path roots are compile errors.
"""
@@builtin type Lens<$S, $A>

@@doc """
Lens module.
Provides compiler-managed path access/update intrinsics:
`view`, `set`, `over`, `over_result`, `compose`.

Typical workflow:

1. Build a path with `Type.segment`, `left / right`, or `Lens::compose(...)`
2. Read with `Lens::view(...)` or `value.segment`
3. Update with `Lens::set(...)`, `Lens::over(...)`, or `Lens::over_result(...)`

## REPL
Struct path create/get/set/update:
xldr(1)> cfg = Config::new("demo")
> cfg: Config = Config { name: "demo", entrypoint: "Main::main", paths: [] }
xldr(2)> cfg.entrypoint
"Main::main"
xldr(3)> cfg2 =? Lens::set(Config.entrypoint, cfg, "App::main")
> cfg2: Config = Config { name: "demo", entrypoint: "App::main", paths: [] }
xldr(4)> cfg3 =? Lens::over(Config.entrypoint, cfg2, {|entry| Ok(entry ++ "!")})
> cfg3: Config = Config { name: "demo", entrypoint: "App::main!", paths: [] }

Tuple path create/get/set/update:
xldr(1)> pair = ("alice", 42)
> pair: (String, Int) = ("alice", 42)
xldr(2)> pair._0
"alice"
xldr(3)> pair2 =? Lens::set(Tuple._1, pair, 99)
> pair2: (String, Int) = ("alice", 99)
xldr(4)> pair3 =? Lens::over(Tuple._1, pair2, {|n| Ok(n + 1)})
> pair3: (String, Int) = ("alice", 100)

Enum path create/get/set/update:
xldr(1)> Lens::view(BitWidth.Any, BitWidth::Any(5))
Ok(5)
xldr(2)> bw2 =? Lens::set(BitWidth.Any, BitWidth::Any(5), 7)
> bw2: BitWidth = BitWidth::Any(7)
xldr(3)> bw3 =? Lens::over(BitWidth.Any, bw2, {|n| Ok(n + 1)})
> bw3: BitWidth = BitWidth::Any(8)

Record path surface:
- create path: `Record.field`
- get: `value.field` or `Lens::view(Record.field, value)`
- set: `Lens::set(Record.field, value, next)`
- update: `Lens::over(Record.field, value, updater)`
- update Result container: `Lens::over_result(Record.field, value, updater)`

Use `docs/site/lens.md` for the longer walkthrough.
"""
@@autoimport
impl Lens {
  @@doc """
  View the focus at `lens` from `source`.
  The declared contract is `Result`-shaped; the compiler refines this in
  Stage1 based on path context.

  How to read the return type:

  - plain field / tuple paths on plain values return plain `A`
  - if `source` is already `Result<S>`, the result stays `Result<A>`
  - if the path contains an enum variant selector such as `Expr.Add`, the result
    is promoted to `Result<A>`

  `value.segment` is shorthand for the same read:
  `cfg.entrypoint`
  `pair._0`

  Variant paths are fallible because the runtime value may be a different
  variant:

  When the selector does not match, the result is `Err(VariantMismatch(...))`.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> cfg = Config::new("demo")
  > cfg: Config = Config { name: "demo", entrypoint: "Main::main", paths: [] }
  xldr(2)> cfg.entrypoint
  "Main::main"

  xldr(1)> pair = ("alice", 42)
  > pair: (String, Int) = ("alice", 42)
  xldr(2)> pair._0
  "alice"

  xldr(1)> Lens::view(BitWidth.Any, BitWidth::Any(5))
  Ok(5)
  """
  @@builtin def view(lens: Lens<$S, $A>, source: $S) -> Result<$A>

  @@doc """
  Replace the focus at `lens` in `source` with `value`.
  Returns the updated source wrapped in `Result`.

  Use `set` when you already have the replacement value.
  When the focus is `Result<$A>`, `set` also accepts a plain `$A` and stores it
  as `Ok($A)`.
  This is especially useful for `T?` fields because `T?` lowers to
  `Result<T, NoneError>`.

  For nested updates, compose the path first:

  If the path contains a variant selector, `set` fails with
  `Err(VariantMismatch(...))` when the current value is not on that variant.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> cfg = Config::new("demo")
  > cfg: Config = Config { name: "demo", entrypoint: "Main::main", paths: [] }
  xldr(2)> cfg2 =? Lens::set(Config.entrypoint, cfg, "App::main")
  > cfg2: Config = Config { name: "demo", entrypoint: "App::main", paths: [] }

  xldr(1)> pair = ("alice", 42)
  > pair: (String, Int) = ("alice", 42)
  xldr(2)> pair2 =? Lens::set(Tuple._1, pair, 99)
  > pair2: (String, Int) = ("alice", 99)

  xldr(1)> bw2 =? Lens::set(BitWidth.Any, BitWidth::Any(5), 7)
  > bw2: BitWidth = BitWidth::Any(7)
  """
  @@builtin def set(lens: Lens<$S, $A>, source: $S, value: $A) -> Result<$S>

  @@doc """
  Update the focus at `lens` in `source` with `update_fun`.
  `update_fun` receives the current focus and returns `Result<$A>`.

  Use `over` when the new value depends on the current focused value.
  When the focus is `Result<$A>`, `over` updates only the successful payload:
  `Ok(value)` calls the updater with `value`, while `Err(err)` skips the updater
  and leaves the field unchanged.
  This makes `Result`-backed fields compose directly with ordinary
  `A -> Result<A>` update helpers.

  `update_fun` must return `Result`.
  If `update_fun` returns `Err(...)`, that error is propagated as-is.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> cfg = Config::new("demo")
  > cfg: Config = Config { name: "demo", entrypoint: "Main::main", paths: [] }
  xldr(2)> cfg2 =? Lens::over(Config.entrypoint, cfg, {|entry| Ok(entry ++ "!")})
  > cfg2: Config = Config { name: "demo", entrypoint: "Main::main!", paths: [] }

  xldr(1)> pair = ("alice", 42)
  > pair: (String, Int) = ("alice", 42)
  xldr(2)> pair2 =? Lens::over(Tuple._1, pair, {|n| Ok(n + 1)})
  > pair2: (String, Int) = ("alice", 43)

  xldr(1)> bw2 =? Lens::over(BitWidth.Any, BitWidth::Any(7), {|n| Ok(n + 1)})
  > bw2: BitWidth = BitWidth::Any(8)
  """
  @@builtin def over(lens: Lens<$S, $A>, source: $S, update_fun: ($A -> Result<$A>)) -> Result<$S>

  @@doc """
  Update a `Result<$A>` focus as a whole.
  `update_fun` receives the entire current `Result<$A>` and returns
  `Result<Result<$A>>`.

  Use `over_result` when you want to rewrite `Ok(...)` and `Err(...)` directly.
  Use plain `over` when you only want to transform the successful payload.

  `update_fun` must return `Result`.
  If `update_fun` returns `Err(...)`, that error is propagated as-is.
  """
  @@builtin def over_result(lens: Lens<$S, Result<$A>>, source: $S, update_fun: (Result<$A> -> Result<Result<$A>>)) -> Result<$S>

  @@doc """
  Compose two lens paths in sequence.
  `compose(outer, inner)` focuses from `S` to `B` via `A`.
  `outer / inner` is equivalent operator sugar.

  Use this for nested access and updates.

  The types must line up:

  - `outer: Lens<S, A>`
  - `inner: Lens<A, B>`
  - result: `Lens<S, B>`

  This is the canonical path-composition API.

  Private field segments keep their visibility rules after composition.
  If one segment is private and the current scope cannot see it, the composed
  path is rejected at compile time.

  ## REPL
  Compose is easiest to try with a user-defined nested type in a source file.
  In the REPL, use the same shape as:
  outer = User.profile
  inner = Profile.name
  path2 = outer / inner
  path = Lens::compose(outer, inner)
  """
  @@builtin def compose(outer: Lens<$S, $A>, inner: Lens<$A, $B>) -> Lens<$S, $B>
}

@@doc """
`Compose` implementation for nested `Lens` paths.
This is the source-level meaning of `left / right` for Lens values.

The compiler still lowers Lens composition at compile time in Stage1, but the
surface contract is expressed through `Compose::compose`.
"""
impl Compose<Lens<$A, $B>, Lens<$S, $B>> for Lens<$S, $A> {
  @@doc """
  Compose the receiver path before `rhs`.
  `left / right` and `Compose::compose(left, right)` follow this contract.
  `Lens::compose(left, right)` remains the canonical helper form.
  """
  @@builtin def compose(self: Self, rhs: Lens<$A, $B>) -> Lens<$S, $B>
}
